issues.problematic_code_snippet:
// Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out
await Task.Yield();

var state = await AuthenticationProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();

private async Task ProcessLogOut(string returnUrl)
{
    if (!ValidateSignOutRequestState())
    {
        Log.LogoutOperationInitiatedExternally(Logger);
        Navigation.NavigateTo(ApplicationPaths.LogOutFailedPath, AuthenticationNavigationOptions with { HistoryEntryState = "The logout was not initiated from within the page." });
        return;
    }

public void Redirect()
{
    if (_tokenResult.InteractionOptions != null)
    {
        _navigation.NavigateToLogin(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl, _tokenResult.InteractionOptions);
    }
    else
    {
        _navigation.NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl!);
    }

findings.body:
The PR description says sign-out validation should "only fail validation when state exists but is invalid". The code does the opposite. `ProcessLogOut` now calls `ValidateSignOutRequestState()` unconditionally. When `HistoryEntryState` is null, `GetCachedNavigationState()` returns null, so `?.Interaction == InteractionType.SignOut` is false and the handler logs and redirects to `LogOutFailedPath`.

Before this change, the null-state case fell back to `SignOutManager.ValidateSignOutState()`. That fallback is gone with the legacy class, and nothing replaces it. Any app that still reaches logout without state (for example through the removed `SetSignOutState` flow) is now always rejected. Either that is the intended outcome, in which case the description and the commit message are wrong, or the null-state case was meant to pass.

::code{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="279-291"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR does not show which behavior is intended. Confirm it with the maintainers, then either restore the null-state pass-through the description promises or correct the description and add a test that locks in the failure.
